  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">AUL</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In this phase, Albinism Umbrella will work to enhance the holistic well-being and socio-economic inclusion of persons with albinismin mainstream development processes. One area of thematic focus will be economic empowerment, under which the project willcarry outSASA training, community scorecard, market assessment and link selected participants to potential employers. It is envisioned that these participants will form groups which will tap into various government programs for sustainability. Two specific districts have been chosen from the East and Central Uganda ( Mukono and Kayunga) basing on the number, of persons with albinism and poverty levels. These will be able to learn from other success districts like Hoima, Lira and Wakiso among others positively impacted. Additionally, to address the needs of persons with albinism around Sexual and Reproductive Health and Rights (SRHR), a team of experienced SRHRtrainers will be recruited to develop and execute capacity-building programs tailored for bothpersons with albinism and healthcare providers. IEC materials focused on addressing their unique SRHR needs and challenges will be designed, printed and distributed, utilizing healthcare facilities, project workshops and health camps to maximize reach. Health outreaches in Kayunga and Mukono will be organized to improve the skin and eye care of persons with albinism and the general community.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ALBINISM UMBRELLA LIMITED</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Albinism Umbrella (AU) was set up with the ultimate aim of having a joint voice for all persons with albinism (PWA) and albinism organization in Uganda. Albinism Umbrella (AU) was founded  in 2014 at the pick of a national out carry for persons with albinism to have a collective voice in order to advocate  their plight in Uganda as a solution to the then limiting factors of the existing albinism organizations. The vision bearer was driven by desire to have lasting impact on the persons with albinism. Theissue of contention among many organizations was either to be classified as a disability movement or not.

AU was legally incorporated in February 2017 as a company limited by guaranty by its founding members Ms. Olive Namutebi and Ms.Phenamyra Nagujjawho were passionate about the wholistic person with albinism.
Albinism Umbrella has grown from strength to strength in the past few years, as a young organization, moving it from a small vision of providing hope to people with albinism to regional outlook. When the organization started it sought to be relevant to the Ugandan community focusing on legislature changes. Now it has a more global perspective on issues of albinism and strives to be the number one authority on issues of albinism and the related challenges in Africa.

Albinism Umbrella is unique in its advocacy strategy that engages all relevant players at national level for instance Parliament, ministerial and local government engagements. The ability and capacity to advocate, analyse and review legal policies.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
